<!-- ============ ABOUT ============ -->
<section id="about" class="sec">
  <div class="wrap agrid">
    <div class="sticky">
      <p class="eyebrow" data-rv>01 · The Story</p>
      <h2 class="sec-title" data-rv>A village at the<br>mouth of the wind</h2>
      <p class="body dropcap" data-rv>Foum Zguid does not announce itself. It appears the way all true things do — gradually, then all at once. A fold in the Anti-Atlas, a line of palms, walls the colour of the evening. For centuries this was the last pause before the great emptiness: caravans watered their camels here, salted their goods, and stepped into the sand sea beyond.</p>
      <p class="body" data-rv class="dly1"><strong>We were born here.</strong> Our guides are sons of these ksours, grandchildren of nomads who read the dunes like scripture. What we offer is not a tour. It is an introduction — to the silence that heals, to tea poured three glasses deep, to a sky that still remembers every star.</p>
      <p class="body" data-rv>Twelve years, thousands of guests, one unchanged promise: <strong>the desert, privately, beautifully, responsibly.</strong></p>
      <ul class="achips" data-rv>
        <li>Tata Province</li><li>Drâa-Tafilalet</li><li>Amazigh Heritage</li><li>Small Groups Only</li><li>Leave-No-Trace</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="acollage" data-rv="right">
      <figure class="ph ph1"><img src="https://image.qwenlm.ai/public_source/59e3c0a3-0889-46ea-884f-ad206b7115d9/1b09f8229-c7e3-4f82-8bd1-56f4fa044093.png" alt="Earthen kasbah houses and date palms of Foum Zguid village at golden hour" loading="lazy"></figure>
      <figure class="ph ph2"><img src="https://image.qwenlm.ai/public_source/59e3c0a3-0889-46ea-884f-ad206b7115d9/1169dedd1-c6e5-4bdc-8690-ed87030faad0.png" alt="Wind-carved dune curves near Foum Zguid" loading="lazy"></figure>
      <div class="quote">"The desert is not empty. It is full of answers."<b>— Nomad proverb</b></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ WHY ============ -->
<section id="why" class="sec light">
  <div class="wrap wgrid">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow" data-rv>02 · Why Foum Zguid</p>
      <h2 class="sec-title" data-rv>The Sahara's<br>best-kept secret</h2>
      <p class="sec-sub" data-rv>While crowds gather elsewhere, the southern erg stays wild, private and unhurried. This is the Morocco discerning travellers whisper about.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="wlist" data-rv>
      <div class="witem"><span class="wnum">01</span><div><h3>The Untouched Erg</h3><p>Erg Chegaga's dunes rise over 100 metres and stretch 40 km — reached only by 4x4 or camel, never by bus, never crowded.</p></div></div>
      <div class="witem"><span class="wnum">02</span><div><h3>True Dark Skies</h3><p>Rated among Morocco's darkest horizons. The Milky Way casts shadows here; meteors are an ordinary Tuesday.</p></div></div>
      <div class="witem"><span class="wnum">03</span><div><h3>Living Nomad Culture</h3><p>Tea with herding families, bread baked in sand ovens, songs older than borders — shared, never staged.</p></div></div>
      <div class="witem"><span class="wnum">04</span><div><h3>Crafted Privacy</h3><p>Maximum six guests per caravan. Your dune crest will have no other footprints but yours.</p></div></div>
      <div class="witem"><span class="wnum">05</span><div><h3>Five-Star Simplicity</h3><p>Plush beds under canvas, private baths, a chef from the oasis — luxury measured in silence, not marble.</p></div></div>
      <div class="witem"><span class="wnum">06</span><div><h3>Conscientious Travel</h3><p>Solar-lit camps, local guides, camel welfare charters. Every dirham stays in the valley.</p></div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

